  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nExpansive surface high pressure centered south of James Bay \nextends southwest into the mid and lower Mississippi Valley \nthis afternoon. Broad subsidence and very dry air below 500 mb\nwill lead to clear skies through tonight. High temperatures to \npeak in the lower 70s today, followed by overnight lows in the\nmid 40s.\n\nA moderating trend begins Wednesday and continues through \nthis weekend as upper level ridging amplifies overhead. Suite of\nmodel guidance indicates 850 hPa temperatures warming to +11C \nto +13C through this period. Afternoon mix-down of this air will\nlead to daily highs in the mid 70s through Friday, then upper\n70s to around 80F this weekend when southwest low level flow\nbecomes better established. Forecast soundings show daytime \nboundary layer mixing reducing afternoon dewpoints into the 40s,\nyielding minimum relative humidity values between 30 and 40 \npercent. Normally in late October this would result in an\nenhanced risk of fire spread, but a weak MSLP gradient will\npromote light winds under 10 mph through Friday.\n\nA transition in the upper air pattern begins Monday into \nTuesday as a potent upper-level trough traverses the northern \nPlains and lifts toward the Upper Midwest. The ECMWF-AI and \noperational ECMWF-Ens have remained consistent with a slightly \nslower frontal passage than the GEFS members, bringing the \nattendant cold front into western Illinois Monday evening and \nclearing southeast Illinois Tuesday afternoon. The NBM \nintroduces slight chance PoPs (15 percent) west of the Illinois\nRiver Monday afternoon. With a lack of upper level support and\nprojected dry airmass, models generally show a dry frontal\npassage for the remainder of the CWA.",
